ding`. Bug premise confirmed.\n- PR head + rc.6 → all three dry-run cases **PASS**, and `go test ./internal/install/...` **exits 0** (install 406.7 s, atomicity 365.5 s). **AC clause 1 met.**\n- PR head + rc.3 pin → **PASS**. No regression at the live pin.\n- Both `default:` branches still fatal (`dryrun_conformance_test.go:292` scope, `:692` effect); the new case is not skipped and no assertion is special-cased.\n- PR 14 CI vs `main` CI: only `Test (windows-latest)` red on **both**, with a byte-identical failing-case set owned by BUG-260731-27h1yc / BUG-260731-fs3dht. **This PR regresses nothing.**\n\n### Why it goes back\n\nDoD item 3 — *prove internal/install against the rc.6 root on macOS, Linux and Windows CI* — was never run. No `candidate-conformance` dispatch exists for this branch, even though rc.6 is an immutable published 40-char ref, the manifest sha256 is already in the evidence artifact, signing was unblocked before the push, and sibling tasks dispatched that same lane the same day.\n\nThat unrun lane hides a real failure. `planEveryProject` drives the **real** `goToolchain`; per fs3dht, `godriver.Probe` rejects the actions/setup-go Windows GOROOT. A failed probe makes `planBuilds` emit `toolchain-unavailable`, which rc.6's `reported_build_outcomes` does not admit. I reproduced the exact mechanism by running the compiled test binary against the rc.6 root with an unresolvable GOROOT:\n\n```\ndryrun_conformance_test.go:291: project 0 reported build outcome \"toolchain-unavailable\",\nwhich the published case does not admit: [cache-hit would-preflight-and-build …]\n```\n\n`internal/install` is never platform-excluded, so under rc.6 it is served on all three runners — meaning **AC clause 2 is currently false on windows-latest**. Not a demand to fix fs3dht here; a demand that the new coupling be verified and declared rather than merged as a silent landmine. Linux is separately unverified (the probe path avoids `probeNativeControls`, so it should be fine, but that is unproven).\n\nAlso flagged, non-blocking: the §2.5 rationale is inverted — §2.5 governs project **lock** order, production `install --all` iterates by **alias** (`cmd/curator/main.go` `sort.Strings(aliases)`), and a dry run takes no lock at all. Nothing asserts order, so no test outcome depends on it, but fidelity-to-production is the whole justification for the binding.\n\n### Recorded\n\n- Outcome artifact `BUG-260731-3a5q1p_review-verdict_RUN-260731-cefd98.md` on the board\n- Full verdict in task notes; checklist items 4, 12, 14 checked\n- Findings appended to `LOGBOOK.md`\n- Status routed `reviewing` → `to-dev`","ttft_ms":2805,"ttft_stream_ms":2653,"time_to_request_ms":31,"type":"result","duration_ms":1001222,"uuid":"a7152b9c-ed3d-4287-8b50-49f6520f3a5d"}
